<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-range" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Range</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Define the range and domain of functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Guidance on the Opening Activity</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To build an intuitive understanding of range as "what comes out" of a function before any algebraic method is introduced. The temperature context is familiar enough for learners to reason about output values without needing formal notation.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Ask learners to share the output values they found in Tasks 1 and 2, then write them on the board. Use the list to ask "is there a lowest value? is there a highest value?" This naturally leads to the idea of a range as a collection of possible outputs. Avoid introducing inequality notation until after the discussion, then use learners' own words to frame the definition.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <activity xml:id="activity-range-temperature">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                A weather station records the temperature in a town throughout the day. The temperature in degrees Celsius at any hour <m>h</m> after midnight is given by the function
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>T(h) = -h^2 + 12h - 20</md>
            </p>
            <p>
                where <m>h</m> ranges from 0 (midnight) to 12 (midday). Think about what output values (temperatures) this function can actually produce over that period.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Calculate the temperature at the following times by substituting each value of <m>h</m> into the function. Record your answers in the table.
                </p>
                <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
                    <row>
                        <cell><alert>Hour <m>h</m></alert></cell>
                        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>6</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>8</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>10</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>12</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><alert>Temperature <m>T(h)</m></alert></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{00}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Looking at the output values in your table, what is the lowest temperature recorded and what is the highest temperature recorded?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the temperature ever exactly <m>0°C</m> during this period? Explain your reasoning.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Based on your table and answers above, describe in words all the temperature values that this function can produce between <m>h = 0</m> and <m>h = 12</m>. These possible output values form the <em>range</em> of the function over this period.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If the domain (the allowed inputs) is all hours from <m>h = 0</m> to <m>h = 12</m>, does changing the domain change the range? Discuss this with a partner.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise the concept of range using the activity as a reference point, and to show learners how to read the range from a graph as well as how to identify it from a rule.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Return to the temperature table. The list of output values learners generated is the range. Emphasise that the range depends entirely on what comes out of the function, not what goes in. Write the range as an inequality (for example, <m>-20 \leq T \leq 16</m>) using the values learners found, before introducing any graphical or algebraic method.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Reading the Range from a Graph:</alert>  Teach learners to scan the graph vertically rather than horizontally. The lowest point gives the minimum output value and the highest point gives the maximum. A filled dot means that value is included, and an open dot means it is not. A horizontal asymptote means the function gets close to a certain output but never reaches it.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Writing the Range:</alert> Encourage learners to write the range as an inequality at this level, for example <m>y \geq 0</m> or <m>-4 \leq y \lt 3</m>, rather than using bracket notation. This keeps the language consistent with how they have been writing the domain.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

   <insight>
        <title>Understanding the Range of a Function</title>
        <p>
            The <term>range of a function</term> is the collection of all output values that the function can actually produce. While the domain tells us which values of <m>x</m> can go into the function, the range tells us which values of <m>y</m> can come out.
        </p>

        <p>
            The table below summarises the range for the main function types you will meet at this level.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-range-rules">
            <title>Range for Common Function Types</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                    <cell>Function Type</cell>
                    <cell>Example</cell>
                    <cell>Range</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Linear (odd-degree)</cell>
                    <cell><m>f(x) = 3x - 5</m></cell>
                    <cell>All real numbers; <m>y</m> can be any value</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Quadratic opening upwards</cell>
                    <cell><m>f(x) = x^2 + 1</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>y \geq 1</m> (output is never below the vertex)</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Quadratic opening downwards</cell>
                    <cell><m>f(x) = -x^2 + 3</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>y \leq 3</m> (output is never above the vertex)</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Square root</cell>
                    <cell><m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>y \geq 0</m> (output is never negative)</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Exponential</cell>
                    <cell><m>f(x) = 2^x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>y \gt 0</m> (output is always positive, never zero)</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Rational with horizontal asymptote</cell>
                    <cell><m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{x}</m></cell>
                    <cell>All real numbers except <m>y = 0</m></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The graphs below show two examples. To read the range from a graph, scan the curve from bottom to top and note the lowest and highest output values reached, keeping an eye out for any horizontal asymptote.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-range-graphs">
            <caption>Reading the range from a graph. The left graph has a minimum output value, while the right graph approaches but never reaches <m>y = 0</m>.</caption>
            <image xml:id="tikz-range-graphs" width="90%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Two graphs used to determine ranges. The first graph is a parabola shifted up by 1 with range y greater than or equal to 1. The second graph is an exponential curve with a horizontal asymptote at y equals 0 and range y greater than 0.
                </shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]

                        % ================= LEFT GRAPH ===================
                        \begin{scope}[shift={(-5,0)}]
                            \draw[->, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.5,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[->, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,5) node[left] {$y$};
                            \draw[blue, thick, domain=-2:2, samples=100]
                                plot (\x, {\x*\x + 1});
                            \filldraw[blue] (0,1) circle (2.5pt);
                            \node at (0,1) [below right] {\small $(0,1)$};
                            \node at (1.5,4.5) {\small $y=x^2+1$};
                            \node[align=center, font=\small] at (0,-2)
                                {Lowest output is at the vertex.\\
                                Range: $y \geq 1$};
                        \end{scope}

                        % ================= RIGHT GRAPH ===================
                        \begin{scope}[shift={(5,0)}]
                            \draw[->, thick] (-4,0) -- (4,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[->, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,4) node[left] {$y$};
                            \draw[dashed, red, thick] (-4,0) -- (4,0)
                                node[above left] {\small asymptote};
                            \draw[red, thick, domain=-4:1.3, samples=100]
                                plot (\x, {exp(\x)});
                            \node at (1.5,3.2) {\small $y=e^x$};
                            \node[align=center, font=\small] at (0,-2)
                                {Curve never touches $y=0$.\\
                                Range: $y \gt 0$};
                        \end{scope}

                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
    </insight>



    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the range of the quadratic function <m>f(x) = x^2 - 4</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The graph of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> is a parabola opening upwards with its lowest point (the vertex) at the origin. Subtracting 4 shifts the entire graph down by 4 units, so the new lowest point is at <m>y = -4</m>. Because the parabola opens upwards and has no highest point, the output can grow without limit above that.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the range is all values of <m>y</m> where <m>y \geq -4</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the range of the rational function <m>g(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the range, we let <m>y = \dfrac{1}{x}</m> and solve for <m>x</m>, giving <m>x = \dfrac{1}{y}</m>. This expression is undefined when <m>y = 0</m>, which means the function can never produce an output of zero. The graph of this function has a horizontal asymptote along the <m>x</m>-axis, confirming that <m>y = 0</m> is never reached.
            </p>
            <p>
                Apart from <m>y = 0</m>, every other real number can be produced as an output. Therefore, the range is all real numbers except <m>y = 0</m>, which we write as <m>y \neq 0</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Calculate the range of the absolute value function <m>h(x) = -|x| + 3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The expression <m>|x|</m> is always greater than or equal to zero for any value of <m>x</m>. Multiplying by <m>-1</m> means <m>-|x|</m> is always less than or equal to zero. Adding <m>3</m> then shifts every output upwards by <m>3</m>, so the highest possible output is <m>0 + 3 = 3</m>, reached when <m>x = 0</m>. Because the graph opens downwards, the output decreases without bound below that.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the range is all values of <m>y</m> where <m>y \leq 3</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine the range of the function <m>p(x)</m> represented by the graph below.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-range-bounded-graph">
                <caption>Graph of a bounded function</caption>
                <image xml:id="tikz-range-bounded-graph" width="70%">
                    <shortdescription>A curve on a coordinate plane with a minimum y-value of -2 and a maximum y-value of 4.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                            % Axes
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-4,0) -- (6,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-3) -- (0,5) node[left] {$y$};
                            
                            % Ticks
                            \draw (0, -2) -- (-0.1, -2) node[left] {$-2$};
                            \draw (0, 4) -- (-0.1, 4) node[left] {$4$};
                            
                            % The curve
                            \draw[very thick, blue] (-2,-2) .. controls (1,5) and (3,-1) .. (5,4);
                            
                            % Endpoints
                            \filldraw[blue] (-2,-2) circle (2.5pt); % Solid dot
                            \filldraw[fill=white, draw=blue, thick] (5,4) circle (2.5pt); % Open dot
                            
                            % Dashed reference lines
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (-2,0) -- (-2,-2) -- (0,-2);
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (5,0) -- (5,4) -- (0,4);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                To find the range from a graph, we scan the curve from bottom to top and note the lowest and highest output values it reaches.
            </p>
            <p>
                The lowest point on the graph is at <m>y = -2</m>. The filled dot at this endpoint confirms that <m>y = -2</m> is included in the range. The highest point the curve reaches is <m>y = 4</m>, but the open dot there tells us that <m>y = 4</m> is not included.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the range is all values of <m>y</m> where <m>-2 \leq y \lt 4</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the range of the rational function <m>q(x)</m> plotted in the graph below.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-range-asymptote-graph">
                <caption>Graph of a function with a horizontal asymptote</caption>
                <image xml:id="tikz-range-asymptote-graph" width="70%">
                    <shortdescription>A rational function graphed in two pieces, showing a horizontal asymptote at y = 1.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
                            % Axes
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-5,0) -- (5,0) node[below] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-4) -- (0,5) node[left] {$y$};
                            
                            % Horizontal Asymptote
                            \draw[dashed, very thick, red] (-5,1) -- (5,1) node[above left] {$y=1$};
                            \draw (0.1, 1) -- (-0.1, 1) node[below left, text=black] {$1$};
                            
                            % Vertical Asymptote
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (-2,-4) -- (-2,5);

                            % The curves
                            \draw[very thick, purple, domain=-5:-2.2, samples=50] plot (\x, {1/(\x+2) + 1});
                            \draw[very thick, purple, domain=-1.8:5, samples=50] plot (\x, {1/(\x+2) + 1});
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The arrows on both ends of the graph show that the curve extends upwards and downwards without limit, meaning the function produces very large and very small output values.
            </p>
            <p>
                However, the red dashed line at <m>y = 1</m> is a horizontal asymptote. The curve gets closer and closer to this line on both sides but never actually reaches it, so the function never produces an output of exactly 1.
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the range is all real numbers except <m>y = 1</m>, which we write as <m>y \neq 1</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

